Caliban inspired many legends about mighty warriors and vengeful spirits in Central America based on the sightings of these Titans by the native people, particularly the Aztecs. {{source|Huntik.com/Profiles|Huntik.com}} Two of the Aztec rulers used two Caliban Amulets in order to conquer their enemies to expand their empires. {{Source|The School of Mystery}}

One Caliban Amulet was later found by the [[Huntik Foundation]] operative [[Simon Judeau]] who used it to save the life of his friend [[Metz]] at the [[Temple of Tecobai]]. {{source|The Divine Comedy}} Metz eventually obtained Simon's Amulet and later gave it to his young charge, [[Dante Vale]], during a trip to Turkey. {{source|Ladies' Choice}} Dante took more than a year to learn how to summon and use Caliban in battle because this Titan is hard to control due to its willful and aggressive nature. {{source|Huntik.com}} Caliban, having powers rivaling even some [[Legendary Titans]], requires a vast amount of energy from his [[Seeker]], and through training, under his old martial arts mentor, [[Master Storm]], Dante went from only being able to summon Caliban for about a minute to being able to summon the Titan for longer durations. {{source|Lok's Leadership}}

Some years later, Dante discovered another Caliban Amulet in [[South America]], during one of his most dangerous missions for the [[Huntik Foundation]]. {{Source|Caliban Amulet (GP Card)|Caliban Amulet}} Dante kept this a secret, with everyone believing he possessed simply the one Caliban, who had become his most distinctive Titan. {{Source|The Mission}} 

During the battle at the [[the Professor's castle]], Dante summoned Caliban to fight Rassimov's [[Ammit Heart-Eater]]. Caliban then fought with [[Thornment]], but was sent back to his Amulet by one of the spiked traps set up by [[Rassimov]]. Later, during the team's showdown with the Professor, Dante was manipulated by Simon into giving back his original Caliban Amulet along with the Rings of [[Behemoth]] and [[Tao]], believing it would help to save [[Metz]]. {{Source|The Divine Comedy}} Dante later revealed to [[Zhalia Moon]] that he had always possessed two Calibans, and invoked his "spare" Caliban to fight and defeat one of the [[Undergolem]] Titans. {{source|The Mission}}

During the battle in [[Klaus' bookshop]] with the newly-returned Rassimov and his [[Blood Spiral]] forces, overpowered and injured, Dante managed to save himself by Powerbonding with Caliban. Powerbonded Caliban used his sword to deflect Rassimov's [[Bladewave]] attack onto [[Wind]] and a [[Silent Soldier]]. He then used one of Dante's moves on [[Harlekin]], throwing him into a [[Marauder]] and taking it out, before defeating Harlekin with his sword. {{source|The Blood Spiral}}

During his secret training in [[China]] under [[Master Storm]], Dante trained with Caliban alongside him with the pair taking on many intense training drills. {{source|Lok's Leadership}}

[[File:S2E43 Dante Caliban.png|thumb|Dante and Powerbonded Caliban train together in perfect sync]]
When the bond with his Seeker became stronger Caliban obtained a stronger appearance with golden armor, longer hair, and an energy blade. Like the other Powerbonded Titans, now he can communicate telepathically with his Seeker. His Powerbonded abilities include:
*'''Breaking Charge''' - enables him to charge at enemies across different terrain including water. {{Source|Zhalia's Mission}}
*'''Leaping Slash''' - enables him to jump through the air and hit an enemy with his sword. {{Source|Den vs Harrison}}
*'''Mirror Fight''' - allows him to fight perfectly in sync with his Seeker. {{Source|Den vs Harrison}}
*'''Rayflector''' - reflects and redirects incoming spells. {{Source|The Power of Umbra}}
*'''Synchronized Strike''' - hits an enemy in perfect sync with his Seeker. {{Source|Den vs Harrison}}
